[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Database Per Service and the Shared Database Anti-Pattern in Microservices"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"Designing a microservices architecture requires careful consideration of data management. The primary rule of data persistence in microservices is the Database Per Service pattern, and violating this leads to the Shared Database anti-pattern."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"Understanding Database Per Service"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"In a true microservices architecture, each microservice must own its private database instance or schema. This ensures loose coupling, allowing teams to develop, deploy, and scale their services independently without impacting other services."},{"order":5,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Loose Coupling: Changes to one service's schema do not break other services."},{"order":6,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Independent Scaling: Services can scale their specific data stores based on load."},{"order":7,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Technology Heterogeneity: Different services can use different database technologies (SQL vs. NoSQL) suited to their specific use cases."},{"order":8,"type":"subheading","text":"The Shared Database Anti-Pattern"},{"order":9,"type":"paragraph","text":"The Shared Database anti-pattern occurs when multiple microservices directly read from and write to the same database instance or schema. While it mimics the simplicity of monolithic architectures, it severely undermines the benefits of microservices."},{"order":10,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Tight Coupling: Services are bound by foreign key constraints and shared tables."},{"order":11,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Deployment Bottlenecks: A schema change requires coordinated deployments across multiple teams."},{"order":12,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Runtime Conflicts: Services can cause deadlocks or performance degradation for one another."},{"order":13,"type":"subheading","text":"Handling Distributed Data"},{"order":14,"type":"paragraph","text":"When adopting the Database Per Service pattern, joining data across services becomes impossible via traditional database SQL joins. Instead, developers must use alternative patterns like API composition or the Saga pattern for distributed transactions."},{"order":15,"type":"code","text":"// Example of API Composition in Node.js
async function getOrderDetails(orderId) {
  const order = await orderService.fetchOrder(orderId);
  const customer = await customerService.fetchCustomer(order.customerId);
  return { ...order, customer };
}"},{"order":16,"type":"subheading","text":"Conclusion"},{"order":17,"type":"paragraph","text":"Avoiding the Shared Database anti-pattern is critical for maintaining service autonomy. By strictly enforcing Database Per Service, organizations can achieve true scalability and organizational agility in their microservices journey."}]
